WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.775
Try it.

2
00:00:00.775 --> 00:00:04.533
Lab 1 runs in the drift-pipeline
folder of the examples,

3
00:00:04.533 --> 00:00:07.038
in a POSIX shell, and nothing signs in.

4
00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:10.762
Step one checks Learn's three
sample workflows offline.

5
00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:15.813
Sample one has five findings and four
warnings, sample two has seven findings

6
00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:20.074
and four warnings, and sample three
has just one: it isn't valid YAML.

7
00:00:20.074 --> 00:00:21.613
The command exits two.

8
00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:26.925
Step two checks the pipeline's own
workflow: zero findings, zero warnings.

9
00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:29.300
Step three prints a cloud's hosts.

10
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:34.247
For DoD, the sign-in authority is
login dot microsoftonline dot u s,

11
00:00:34.247 --> 00:00:37.819
the Graph host is dod-graph
dot microsoft dot u s,

12
00:00:37.819 --> 00:00:41.575
and the Azure environment
is Azure U S Government.

13
00:00:41.575 --> 00:00:47.124
Microsoft Learn's page, Microsoft
Graph national cloud deployments,

14
00:00:47.124 --> 00:00:50.337
lists those GCC High and DoD Graph hosts.

15
00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:54.906
That's the trap: the Azure environment
covers both GCC High and DoD,

16
00:00:54.906 --> 00:00:58.037
so the Graph host has to
come from the profile.

17
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:00.858
Then ask for a name it
doesn't know, u s gov.

18
00:01:00.858 --> 00:01:05.060
It's refused, with exit code
one, never mapped to worldwide.
